{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at the end of days, and Cain brought of the fruit of the ground an offering to HaShem.</b> Scripture does not explain what 'at the end of days' means, and when those days began concerning which the end arrived. And it appears fitting to say by way of remez, that Cain and Abel differed concerning what constitutes the perfection of man: whether this world and its successes are the ultimate perfection of man with no perfection beyond it, or whether there is yet another world, eternal. And as it appears, since Cain was a lover of land (אֲדָמָה), he held that there is no reckoning in the grave and this world leaves nothing behind, therefore he chose for his portion all the desires of this world and its successes, thinking that 'the advantage of land is in everything.' And Abel held that there is yet another eternal world in which man attains the ultimate end, therefore he chose to be a shepherd of sheep, which brings about isolation, as many prophets did — such as Moses and David and others — and in order to bring from them an offering to HaShem. And perhaps they made a division between them, as is found in the Midrash (Exodus Rabbah 31:18) concerning Esau and Jacob, who made a division between them: for Esau took for his portion all the desires of this world, as he said, 'Let my lord pass on before his servant,' meaning, let him take his portion first; and Jacob chose for his portion the eternal world, and his hand grasps the heel of Esau, that is, at the end of Esau's dominion. So too Cain and Abel made this division between them. And this is the intent of the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 22:16) that says that Cain took land for his portion and Abel took movable goods, etc. — they thereby indicated what was in their hearts regarding the division of the reward of this world and the World to Come. For all the desires of this world — a man cannot carry them off and take from them anything to bring them across to the other world of the descent; rather they are like ground standing in its place. It is like one who goes to a fair and buys there with his money lands and houses that he cannot carry with him to his home, and he has from them only what he eats of their fruit while yet in a foreign land; and when he goes home he returns empty, and nothing does he bear of his labor — what he built and what he planted — and he leaves for others wealth and a wall which he founded. So too one who makes this world his principal — he has from it nothing but a palm's-narrow grasp of enjoyment, felt from hands full of toil and pursuit of wind; and he uses them only for the duration of his sojourn in this world, for his brief stay in the house, like a stranger and guest tarrying to lodge; and nothing does he bring with him to the eternal world, for there is his home. And this is the view of Cain and his fellows, lovers of temporal acquisitions. And Abel — he renders all temporal acquisitions vanity (מַהְבִּיל), for they are discipline of vanities (הֲבָלִים); he chose for himself movable goods, and this is the fulfillment of the commandments of HaShem, blessed be He, which a man can carry with him from the place where he stands as a guest and sojourner to the place where he is a native-born citizen, for there a home shall be made for him." "And it is well known that all lovers of the acquisition of wealth never perform any commandment or charity from their money all the days of their lives, with this device which they devise: 'Behold, the day is still long here — let us eat to satiety, let us use our money.' And as long as they see themselves strong and healthy, and their former strength is their strength now, they give nothing of their wealth and property for the service of HaShem, thinking 'Who will eat and who will enjoy besides me?' But at the end of the days of their life, when they see themselves drawing near to the gates of death and realize that in his death he shall not take everything, then a spirit of jealousy passes over him to be jealous of his fellows who do the matter of HaShem, and he too does a little from the very smallest portion and consents to bring an offering to HaShem — whether by way of sacrifice, whether to give to the poor, or in some other way. And this is 'one who lends to the poor in his hour of distress (דָּחְקוֹ),' meaning the distress of the lender — that the hour presses him to do, as will be explained (Deuteronomy 31:17) with the help of HaShem. And even this, when he is near the gates of death, he does not give for the sake of the commandment except the smallest portion of his wealth — a pittance — and he picks out the dross and sends it as an offering to HaShem. And every work that is despised and melted away — this is the portion of the Most High; and every good shoulder and cut — that is his portion. For his hands refuse to touch the choice part, and his eye is grudging with his own to give a fitting portion, an offering to HaShem.\n<br>Therefore it is said of Cain, who was of these classes, 'And it came to pass מִקֵּץ יָמִים' — that is, the end of the days of his life, either literally at their conclusion or the mindfulness of their conclusion is what stirred in him this slight awakening, for he felt within himself that there would be an end to all his toil in this world. And had he not felt within himself that an end would come to all flesh and did not much remember the days of his life, then he would never have brought any offering to HaShem. And even so, this mindfulness of the end of days availed him only to separate out the least and the worst, that is, flax seed. And this is the intent of the hidden Midrash that says Cain was מִסִּטְרָא בִּישָׁא (from the side of evil); therefore it says מִקֵּץ יָמִים and not מִקֵּץ יָמִין. Now I have heard but do not understand the intent of this Midrash — what is the difference between יָמִים and יָמִין? Surely his intent is to say that Cain did not bring his offering for the sake of the true ultimate end stored away for the righteous — לְקֵץ הַיָּמִין — in the World to Come, as it is said (Daniel 12:13), 'And you shall stand for your lot לְקֵץ הַיָּמִין,' since Cain denied the resurrection of the dead. Rather, 'מִקֵּץ יָמִים' — he brought it thinking that in any case there will be an end to all his toil and nothing would he carry with him; this is what distinguished his act, as stated.\n<br>And this is the intent of the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 22:5) which says a parable of Cain: like a servant who ate the first fruits and sent the ends (סְיָפוֹת) to the king. That is to say, in his youth — that is, the first days, which have the prerogative of firstborn and first — he then eats of his wealth as much as he finds in his power to eat, and he sends to the King, the King of the world, blessed be He, the סְיָפוֹת, meaning the end of the days of his life; for those days about which he says, 'I have no desire in them' — and no craving or physical lust — those he sends to the King to perform in them the service of HaShem, which is foreign in his eyes. And סְיָפוֹת is מִקֵּץ יָמִים, which he mentioned." "And I saw in the book Tzror haMor, and likewise the R.E.M. (Rabbi Elhanan ben Merari) explained, that קָרְבָּן is the final letters of פִּשְׁתָּן — when you write קוֹ״ף רֵי״שׁ בֵּי״ת נוּ״ן. And according to their words, this Midrash may be explained: like a servant who ate the first fruits and sent the ends (סְיָפוֹת) to the king — for the בַּכּוּרוֹת (first fruits) is the first letters of the word קָרְבָּן, meaning the thing worthy of drawing near (לְקָרְבָּן), which is the choice part; and the סְיָפוֹת (ends) is the final letters, which is פִּשְׁתָּן. And Abel brought of the firstborn of his flock and of their fat — not from mindfulness of the end and the conclusion of the days of his life, but for the sake of the true ultimate end, to bring sustenance to his eternal home לְקֵץ הַיָּמִין, like the way of one who travels far who does not make much of his eating so long as he is a guest, but sends the choice portion to his eternal home. Therefore 'HaShem turned to Abel and to his offering,' meaning 'to Abel' — for his intent was pleasing to Him, blessed be He; 'and to his offering' — for the deed too was pleasing. And this matter is explicit at the beginning of the offerings, where it is said (Leviticus 1:2), 'When a man of you brings an offering to HaShem' — when he wishes to bring an offering that will be accepted to HaShem, then he should do as Abel did, who brought of the firstborn of his flock and of their fat: so too should he bring from the animal, from the cattle and from the sheep, etc. — that is, the firstborn of his flock. And what it says, 'you shall bring your offering' — that is וּמֵחֶלְבֵיהֶן, meaning the choice part that you select for yourself for your own eating, for without doubt you would choose for yourself every good vegetable — shoulder and cut; this is called קָרְבַּנְכֶם (your offering) that you bring to yourself and desire; and that choice portion you shall bring to the Most High." "And as for what is written, <b>'And Abel also brought, he too.'</b> What is גַּם (also)? And I saw in the book Tzror haMor that it blames Abel somewhat for not being stirred on his own to the offering, for he did not bring it until after he saw Cain going and offering; and therefore it says 'And Abel brought גַּם הוּא' — with the word גַּם it hints that he delayed his offering and brought it from the side of jealousy toward his brother. And it may be that concerning one who acts from the side of jealousy, Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 4:4), 'And I saw all toil and all skill of work, that it is a man's jealousy of his fellow — גַּם זֶה הֶבֶל,' meaning, this is the גַּם in which Abel stumbled, as it says 'והבל הביא גם הוא.' And it seems to me to bring a kind of support for his words, for it says, 'And it came to pass while they were in the field, that Cain rose against Abel his brother and killed him' — Scripture mentions 'field' because there was a dispute between them concerning the offerings, and each one cast aspersion on the offering of the other, as is the way of two men who have a quarrel: for Abel blames Cain because he brought from the worst — flax seed; and Cain blames Abel because he was not stirred to the act on his own but only after he saw Cain's offering — a spirit of jealousy came over him and he wished to match him too. And he said to him: 'Since you are not stirred to a good deed from yourself but only when you see others doing it — if so, rest while you sit among your people; but when you are in the field, in a place where no one else is present, then surely you would never do anything good. And if so, you are not worthy to be in the world, for HaShem has no desire in fools and many vanities — those who act from the side of jealousy and not for the sake of Heaven.' And with this Cain defeated Abel; therefore it says, 'And it came to pass while they were in the field.' When he answered him a victorious retort from the side of the field, then 'Cain rose against Abel his brother and killed him,' for the field is the place of the sin, and there shall be his burial." "And what they said — that the offering of the first man had a single horn on its forehead (Hullin 60a) — they intended by this that since the first man was alone in the world, that is, one horn (קֶרֶן אֶחָד); if so, surely he did not learn the act from any man but was stirred to the act from himself; therefore his offering was accepted. Therefore it says, 'When a man (אָדָם) brings an offering to HaShem' — when he is like the first man (אָדָם הָרִאשׁוֹן), who was stirred to the offering from himself. And this is what it says, 'כִּי יַקְרִיב מִכֶּם' — meaning, from you and from within you shall come the stirring; this is an offering to HaShem, and not 'a man's jealousy of his fellow.' And this will be explained further (Leviticus 1:2) with the help of HaShem."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The wordplay יָמִים ('days') vs. יָמִין ('right [side]') depends on the additional rabbinic sense of יָמִין as the 'right side,' i.e., the side of merit; the Kli Yakar unpacks it via לְקֵץ הַיָּמִין ('the end of the right [side]'), a phrase from Daniel 12:13.", 2 "The acrostic קרב״ן (qof-resh-bet-nun) spells קָרְבָּן; its 'final letters' reading requires taking פִּשְׁתָּן and extracting the consecutive letters ק-ר-ב-ן from it — the reading of the Tzror haMor preserved here. 'R.E.M.' (הָרְאֵ״ם) is left as transmitted; identity uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:41.020829604Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}